WEBVTT
TITLE File I/O in C: the Stream, the Six Modes and the Return Value That Decides — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
A FILE pointer is a handle to a stream, not the file itself.

2
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:12.000
The record holds a position, a buffer, an end flag and an error flag.

3
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:18.000
Three streams are already open when main starts: stdin, stdout, stderr.

4
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:24.000
The buffer sits between your program and the disk. The bytes are in one place or the other.

5
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:30.000
So a file question is really four questions: open, position, count, close.

6
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:36.000
A mode string says three things: what you may do, where you start, and what happens to the old content.

7
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:42.000
On an existing file of four bytes: r opens at 0, w empties it, a opens at the end.

8
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:48.000
On a missing file: r and r+ fail; w, w+, a and a+ create it.

9
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:54.000
The plus adds the other direction. It never rescues a file that does not exist.

10
00:00:54.000 --> 00:01:00.000
b changes nothing on a text stream question, and everything on Windows.

11
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:06.000
fopen returns a null pointer when it cannot open. It does not return an empty stream.

12
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:12.000
Using that pointer is undefined behaviour: a crash, or worse, a quiet wrong answer.

13
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:18.000
The check is one line, and it must come before the first use.

14
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:24.000
An empty file is not a failed open: it opens, and the first read reports the end.

15
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:30.000
perror reads the reason from errno; the return value alone does not say why.

16
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:36.000
fgetc returns the next byte as an int, or EOF when there is no next byte.

17
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:42.000
That is why the result is stored in an int: all 256 byte values are legal, and EOF is not one of them.

18
00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:48.000
Store it in a char and a byte of value 255 becomes EOF. The loop stops early.

19
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:54.000
The correct loop reads into an int and stops on EOF, never on feof.

20
00:01:54.000 --> 00:02:00.000
feof answers a question about the last read, so testing it first asks too early.

21
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:06.000
fgets takes a size, reads at most one less than it, and always terminates.

22
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:12.000
It keeps the newline, so a line ends in a newline and a zero, not a zero alone.

23
00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:18.000
A short buffer splits a line, and the rest arrives on the next call.

24
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:24.000
It returns the buffer, or a null pointer at the end or on an error.

25
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:30.000
The buffer is not cleared on failure, so the last line is printed twice.

26
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:36.000
fprintf returns the number of characters written, or a negative value on failure.

27
00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:42.000
A loop writing one number and a newline for one to ten writes twenty-one bytes.

28
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:48.000
fscanf returns how many items it assigned. A short count is the failure.

29
00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:54.000
fread and fwrite count elements, not bytes. Read the short count as an error.

30
00:02:54.000 --> 00:03:00.000
The count is the return value. The data is in the argument, which is why the check comes first.

31
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:06.000
ftell reports the position as a long, counted from the start of the file.

32
00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:12.000
fseek takes an origin: the start, the current position, or the end.

33
00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:18.000
rewind is fseek to the start, and it also clears the error flag.

34
00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:24.000
In a text stream only a zero or an ftell offset is portable; anything else is implementation-defined.

35
00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:30.000
fseek reports failure with a nonzero return; a successful seek clears the end flag.

36
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:36.000
In append mode every write goes to the end, wherever the position is.

37
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:42.000
Seek to zero, write one character, and the character lands after the old content.

38
00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:48.000
a+ reads from the start and writes at the end. The two positions are not one number.

39
00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:54.000
Append never destroys the old content, and w never keeps it.

40
00:03:54.000 --> 00:04:00.000
A log file is the reason the rule exists: two writers never overwrite each other.

41
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:06.000
In an update stream a write must be flushed or followed by a seek before a read.

42
00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:12.000
Skip the seek and the program is not defined. It is not "reads the old byte".

43
00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:18.000
Add the seek and the same program becomes defined, and its output is fixed.

44
00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:24.000
While the stream is buffered, written bytes are in the buffer and not on the disk.

45
00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:30.000
fflush on an input stream is undefined in C, and a no-op on POSIX.

46
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:36.000
fclose writes out the buffer, then releases the stream.

47
00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:42.000
It returns zero, or EOF when the flush failed. That return is the last report you get.

48
00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:48.000
Exiting normally closes every open stream; a crash does not.

49
00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:54.000
The five-step procedure: check the open, know the position, check the count, position between directions, check the close.

50
00:04:54.000 --> 00:05:00.000
Nothing here is a remembered number: the file on the canvas is simulated byte by byte.

